Re-reading I meant to illustrate the difference between these two:
```
select * from foo where name = '';
select * from foo where name IS NULL;
```
Negating an empty string (`content!=''`) returns non-null/non-empty but when checking for an empty string (`content=''`) it'll return non-null/empty. I've been trying to get a query to break without `<>` but `!=''` seems to be doing the right thing. If I can pinpoint where/why `<>` was needed I'll bring it up again. http://stackoverflow.com/questions/14575807/how-to-select-non-empty-columns-in-mysql is a reference to null/empty checking with `<>`.

