I've been playing with CloudFiles checking out current perf using Azure storage. I know this is work in progress, just wanted to get a feeling for where we are, and to open a discussion about how to set expectations correctly and where future work may be done.

Here's the script I've been using with timings shown using my local-redundant storage on a 2 machine (small-instance?) cluster (presumably machine size doesn't matter much).  1, 10, 100, 1000 refer to MB, results are in seconds.  Lots of caveats: some results may be heavily affected by caching, and all results may be affected by other activity on the VMs and in the data centre as a whole, and the storage options on the storage account etc. etc.

I assume the overall level of I/O perf is far from competitive with dedicated map/reduce systems that have direct access to disks (and schedulers which place work directly on the right partition), but would be reasonably competitive with other systems based on Azure storage. Comparisons with AWS would be interesting too.

I'd like to understand the data-locality model better - is it possible to request that processes run close to data, or is caching of immutable data the technique being relied on to get data perf?

You definitely notice the affects of caching with occasional results like this (8s --> 1s)
```
// #1 [(1, 0.3036431); (10, 0.397323); (100, 8.2052502)]
// #2   [(1, 0.3057246); (10, 0.3347746); (100, 1.7994133)]
