Hi Paul and Ernest,
Here's a simplest example for why I would not like to use static compile:
You may have the following rules in your common .excss file:
  @var fg white;
  @var bg blue;
  #foo {
    color: $fg;
    background-color: $bg;
  }

And when changing theme, you just need to modify the value of the two variables:
  CSS.setVariable("fg", "pink");
  CSS.setVariable("bg", "purple");

But if you do the pre static compile for the excss file to make it into css, 
the "setVariable" won't work on the fly.

And I didn't see an obvious latency when switching to excss.

If we would like to promote experimental-css, we'd better not only publicize 
the hierarchical writing mode, but also broadcast the dynamic behavior on the 
run time.

That's I still would like to expose the .excss to our users.

Thank!
```

